# Forbidden patterns for Java files (*.java). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: java
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB - patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation.
# Patterns are POSIX ERE, matched case-sensitively with `grep -anE`.
# Bans console debug output (keeps logger.info/warn/error intact) plus two
# narrow command-execution / TLS sinks below.
# printStackTrace ban is no-arg only; printStackTrace(writer) is allowed (logging idiom).
# Installed as `.forbidden-patterns/java.txt`.

(^|[^A-Za-z_])System\.out\.print(ln)?[[:space:]]*\(	Use a logger (slf4j/Log4j), not System.out.print/println
(^|[^A-Za-z_])System\.err\.print(ln|f)?[[:space:]]*\(	Use logger.error(), not System.err.print/println/printf
\.printStackTrace[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*\)	Log the exception via the logger, not e.printStackTrace(); pass a PrintWriter to capture it instead

# --- Security: command execution / TLS validation ---
# Java ships no linter template, no hook lint and a placeholder CI job, so this
# file is the only gate in front of these lines. Each rule is deliberately much
# narrower than the vulnerability class it names: a miss costs one finding, a
# false positive fails a build in someone else's repo on code they did not
# write. On the ~19k-file first pass both rules were silent. On the 37.9k-file
# latest pass they fire four times in total - Runtime.exec once (ant) and
# checkServerTrusted three times (testcontainers) - and all four hits are TRUE
# positives: real built-command exec and real empty-body TrustManagers. Zero
# false positives on either pass. Corpus: BouncyCastle, Netty, HttpClient,
# Spring, Hibernate, Tomcat, Jetty, Kafka, ZooKeeper, Guava, commons-*,
# Jackson, Mockito, JUnit, ant, testcontainers - library AND test sources.
#
# Runtime.exec: only a CONCATENATED or String.format()-BUILT command string -
# the `+` branch stops at `)`, `,` and `{` so it cannot reach inside the
# fixed-argv forms. A bare literal `exec("ls -la")` does NOT fire: it is a
# fixed command, not a built one, and the message would be wrong about it.
# Do not widen the `+` branch back to `[^)]*\+`: unanchored, it matched the
# `+` inside `exec(new String[]{javaHome + "/bin/java", ...})` and so blocked
# the very fixed-argv shape this rule's own message tells people to use.
# Deliberately missed: `exec(cmd)` with a bare variable, a receiver held in a
# `Runtime` local, a call split across lines, and a concatenation whose leading
# literal contains `)`, `,` or `{`.
#
# checkServerTrusted: only a DECLARATION whose body is EMPTY on the same line -
# `{}` / `{ }`, optionally after a `throws` clause. It is a TRIPWIRE, not
# coverage: measured against real trust-all TrustManagers it catches about 17%
# of them - the other ~83% write the empty body across two or more lines
# (`{ //nop }` or a comment on its own line) and the scan is line-at-a-time.
# Do not describe this rule as catching trust-all TrustManagers generally.
# What it does not fire on is the point: delegating, wrapping and pinning
# implementations, interface declarations, a body that merely contains `{}`,
# and calls to `delegate.checkServerTrusted(...)` - including inside a one-line
# `try { ... } catch (CertificateException e) {}`.
#
# Removed after testing, do not re-add as written: `\.readObject[[:space:]]*\(\)`.
# `readObject()` is also the ASN.1, PEM and ObjectInput reader API and the body
# of every custom `private void readObject` hook - 367 hits over the corpus,
# essentially all benign. Line-level regex cannot tell those from a
# java.io.ObjectInputStream fed untrusted bytes.
#
# Removed after testing, do not re-add as written:
# `(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER[[:space:]]*[),;]`. It caught
# nothing: zero hits across 37.9k real Java files. What it did match is English
# prose, which supplies the trailing `,` or `;` just as code does - including
# the security-hardening comment that tells the next person to remove the
# constant ("remove ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER, it disables checks;"). A rule
# with no measured catch and a demonstrated false positive on its own advice is
# net-negative in someone else's pre-commit hook. Requiring a `[.(]` prefix
# does drop the prose hits, but there is still nothing measured for it to
# catch, so the whole rule is gone rather than narrowed.
(^|[^A-Za-z_])Runtime\.getRuntime[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*\)[[:space:]]*\.exec[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*("[^"]*"[[:space:]]*\+|String\.format|[^),{]*\+)	Runtime.exec() on a built command string is a command-injection sink (it also splits the string on whitespace) - use ProcessBuilder with a fixed argv
(^|[^A-Za-z_])checkServerTrusted[[:space:]]*\([^)]*\)[[:space:]]*(throws[[:space:]][A-Za-z0-9_,.[:space:]]*)?\{[[:space:]]*\}	An EMPTY checkServerTrusted() accepts every certificate - that is TLS validation switched off (MITM risk); validate the chain or keep the platform TrustManager

# Optional (opt-in) — uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(TODO|FIXME|XXX)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	TODO/FIXME/XXX not allowed - open a ticket instead
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])System\.out\.printf[[:space:]]*\(	Use a logger, not System.out.printf (FP-prone in CLI tools)
# Java deserialization ships OFF. `new ObjectInputStream(...)` on untrusted
# bytes is RCE, but the regex cannot see where the bytes came from, and in real
# code most constructions are serialization round-trip TESTS and deep-copy /
# session-cache helpers reading bytes the same process just wrote (24 of the 35
# corpus hits). Enable it in a module that should never use Java serialization
# at all, and expect to mark its round-trip tests with scaffold-allow.
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])new[[:space:]]+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\.)*ObjectInputStream[[:space:]]*\(	Java deserialization of untrusted bytes is RCE (CVE-2015-4852 class) - use JSON/protobuf with a schema
# setAccessible(true) ships OFF: it is a design smell, not a sink, and it is
# ordinary in Jackson/Spring/Hibernate glue, JUnit ReflectionTestUtils-style test
# helpers and any DI container (113 corpus hits across 65 files) - an always-on
# rule blocks correct commits in every one of those. Enable it in an
# application module that should contain no reflection of its own.
# \.setAccessible[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*true[[:space:]]*\)	setAccessible(true) defeats access control and final-field safety - drop the reflection (off by default: routine in framework glue and tests)
